#--: Overview

The declarations themselves live in #~"lib/system/os.z", which is the one
place they are written. Every definition, field, arm, generic parameter and
own-line parameter there carries its own doc comment, and #~"zls" surfaces
each on hover; the corpus runner ratchets that coverage under #~"make ci".
This page is the prose and the worked examples -- read it for how the unit
is meant to be used, and the source for the exact surface.

The #~"os" unit exposes process-level primitives: argv,
environment variables, working directory, process identity,
platform / architecture descriptors, and subprocess spawning.
Paired with #~"io" -- where
#~"io" surfaces failures as #~"Result t: T e: IoError", #~"os"
primitives are either infallible or use #~"Option" when absence
is expected.

Accessed via qualified names only -- #~"os.args", #~"os.env
...", #~"os.exit ..."; there is no unqualified re-export.

#b:"Design principles."

#ul:
  [#*: #b:"Option vs. Result by semantic." If absence is normal,
    return #~"Option(T)" (#~"env"). If a syscall can fail,
    return #~"Result t: T e: IoError" (#~"setEnv", #~"cwd"). No
    separate #~"os.error" -- errno domain is shared with #~"io",
    and callers often interleave env mutation with file I/O]
  [#*: #b:"Ambient process state is implicit." #~"os.args",
    #~"os.cwd", #~"os.pid" take no handle. Mirrors #~"io.stdin"
    / #~"io.stdout" / #~"io.stderr"]
  [#*: #b:"Caller owns returned heap." Every function returning
    a #~"String" or #~"List" yields freshly-allocated storage.
    Scope-exit runs the destructor]
  [#*: #b:"Native on each system." On POSIX the natives use libc
    (#~"setenv", #~"environ", #~"getcwd", #~"posix_spawnp",
    #~"getpwuid_r", ...); on Windows the Win32 calls, in UTF-16
    (#~"GetEnvironmentVariableW", #~"CreateProcessW",
    #~"GetUserNameW", ...). What a caller sees is the same, but where
    the system's own notion differs: a path the runtime returns is
    native (#~"C:\\Users\\x"), environment names match regardless
    of case on Windows, #~"homeDir" is #~"%USERPROFILE%" there, and
    #~"args" comes from the command line itself, split as the C
    runtime splits it]


#--: Index

#p: #b:"Argv"

#ul:
  [#*: [#> to=args: #b:"os.args"] -- #~"function out (List T: String)"]

#p: #b:"Environment"

#ul:
  [#*: [#> to=get-env: #b:"os.env"] -- #~"function {key: StringView} out (Option T: String)"]
  [#*: [#> to=set-env: #b:"os.setEnv"] -- #~"function {key: StringView value: StringView} out (Result T: null E: IoError)"]
  [#*: [#> to=unset-env: #b:"os.unsetEnv"] -- #~"function {key: StringView} out (Result T: null E: IoError)"]
  [#*: [#> to=env-names: #b:"os.envNames"] -- #~"function out (List T: String)"]

#p: #b:"Working directory"

#ul:
  [#*: [#> to=cwd: #b:"os.cwd"] -- #~"function out (Result T: String E: IoError)"]
  [#*: [#> to=exe-path: #b:"os.exePath"] -- #~"function out (Result T: String E: IoError)"]
  [#*: [#> to=set-cwd: #b:"os.setCwd"] -- #~"function {Path: StringView} out (Result T: null E: IoError)"]
  [#*: [#> to=temp-dir: #b:"os.tempDir"] -- #~"function out String"]
  [#*: [#> to=null-device: #b:"os.nullDevice"] -- #~"function out String"]

#p: #b:"Process identity"

#ul:
  [#*: [#> to=pid: #b:"os.pid"] -- #~"function out i32"]
  [#*: [#> to=ppid: #b:"os.ppid"] -- #~"function out i32"]
  [#*: [#> to=user-name: #b:"os.userName"] -- #~"function out (Result T: String E: IoError)"]
  [#*: [#> to=home-dir: #b:"os.homeDir"] -- #~"function out (Result T: String E: IoError)"]
  [#*: [#> to=hostname: #b:"os.hostname"] -- #~"function out (Result T: String E: IoError)"]

#p: #b:"Build descriptors"

#ul:
  [#*: [#> to=build-commit: #b:"os.buildCommit"] -- #~"function out String"]
  [#*: [#> to=build-date: #b:"os.buildDate"] -- #~"function out String"]
  [#*: [#> to=build-compiler: #b:"os.buildCompiler"] -- #~"function out String"]

#p: #b:"Process control"

#ul:
  [#*: [#> to=exit: #b:"os.exit"] -- #~"function {code: i32}"]
  [#*: [#> to=spawn: #b:"os.spawn"] -- #~"function {argv: (List T: String) cwd: StringView outPath: StringView errPath: StringView timeoutSecs: i32} out i32"]
  [#*: [#> to=spawn-async: #b:"os.spawnAsync"] -- #~"function {argv: (List T: String) cwd: StringView outPath: StringView errPath: StringView} out (Result T: Child E: IoError)"]
  [#*: [#> to=wait-any: #b:"os.waitAny"] -- #~"function {children: List Child} out procexit"]
  [#*: [#> to=kill: #b:"Child.kill"] -- #~"function {:this}"]
  [#*: [#> to=sleep-millis: #b:"os.sleepMillis"] -- #~"function {ms: i64}"]
  [#*: [#> to=monotonic-millis: #b:"os.monotonicMillis"] -- #~"function out i64"]


#--: args


Program argument vector as passed to #~"main". Position 0 is the
program name (as the kernel / shell provided it); subsequent
elements are the explicit arguments.

The returned list and every string inside are freshly-allocated
and owned by the caller; scope exit frees both. Repeated calls
each re-allocate -- cache the result if you iterate multiple
times.

#code language=zerolang: """
  a: os.args
  for each arg: a.listView loop { print arg }
"""


#--: get_env


Look up environment variable #~"key". Returns #~"Option.some
value" when the variable is set (even to the empty string);
#~"Option.none" when the variable is not present.

Missing-env is #~"Option" (not #~"IoError") because it is an
expected everyday condition.

#code language=zerolang: """
  home: os.env key: "HOME"
  match (
      home
  ) case some then {
      print home
  } case none then {
      print "/tmp"
  }
"""


#--: set_env


Insert or overwrite an environment variable in the current
process. #~"setenv(key, value, 1)" under the hood -- always
overwrites.

Errors surface as #~"IoError"; the most common is #~"other"
(EINVAL on a malformed key). The errno domain is shared with
#~"io" since env mutation often interleaves with file I/O.

In a program that starts threads (#~"thread.spawn"), it is refused while
another thread runs -- #~"IoError.other", saying so: changing the environment
may move it under a read on that thread, and POSIX promises neither call is
safe then. Set what the threads need before the first #~"spawn", or after the
last #~"join".


#--: unset_env


Remove #~"key" from the environment. Succeeds whether or not
#~"key" was previously set; errors only on an invalid key name
(EINVAL surfaces as #~"IoError.other").
Refused while another thread runs, as #~"os.setEnv" is.


#--: env_names


Snapshot of every environment variable name in the current
process, in #~"environ" order. The returned list and each of
its entries are freshly-allocated; scope exit frees both.

Values are omitted from the snapshot to avoid an O(n) string
copy for callers that filter first; use #~"os.env" to fetch
each value.

#code language=zerolang: """
  names: os.envNames
  for each n: names.listView loop {
      v: os.env key: n.stringView
      match (
          v
      ) case some then {
          print n
          print v
      } case none then null    # unset since the snapshot; skip
  }
"""


#--: cwd


Current working directory as an absolute path. Typically
succeeds; errors only when the cwd inode has been unlinked or
permission to resolve it has been revoked.


#--: exe_path


Absolute path of the running executable. On Linux this reads
#~"/proc/self/exe"; on macOS, #~"_NSGetExecutablePath"; on Windows,
#~"GetModuleFileNameW", a native path. Lets a
program locate its own install tree -- the compiler uses it to find
its bundled stdlib and runtime when run from an arbitrary directory.
Errors only if the platform link read fails.


#--: set_cwd


#~"chdir" to #~"Path". Affects subsequent relative-path I/O
calls -- in #b:"every" thread: the working directory belongs to the
process, not to a thread. Fails with the usual #~"IoError" variants
(#~"notfound", #~"notdir", #~"permissiondenied").


#--: temp_dir


The directory for temporary files: #~"$TMPDIR", else #~"/tmp"; on
Windows #~"GetTempPathW"'s (#~"%TMP%", #~"%TEMP%", #~"%USERPROFILE%",
else the Windows directory), a native path such as
#~"C:\\Users\\x\\AppData\\Local\\Temp". It never ends in a separator
unless it is a root. Infallible.


#--: null_device


The device that discards what is written to it and reads as empty:
#~"/dev/null", or #~"NUL" on Windows -- the path to hand
#~"os.spawn" to throw a stream away. Answered at compile time, from
#~"z.build.os".


#--: cpu_count


Online processors, for sizing a worker pool. Never returns less than
1, so a caller may divide by it; a host that cannot answer reads as
#~"1", which means #b:"run serially" rather than #b:"run zero jobs".
Clamped above at 1024. Infallible.

#--: pid


Current process id. POSIX #~"pid_t" is #~"int" on Linux;
narrowed to #~"i32". Infallible.


#--: ppid


Parent process id. Infallible. Windows keeps no parent link to ask
for, so there it is the parent id this process's entry in a Toolhelp
snapshot records: #~"0" when no snapshot can be taken, and when the
parent is not a Windows process (one started from Linux under wine).


#--: user_name


Login name of the effective user, resolved via the passwd
database (#~"getpwuid_r(geteuid())"); on Windows, the account the
process runs as (#~"GetUserNameW"). Errors when the uid has
no passwd entry or the database is unreadable.


#--: home_dir


The effective user's home directory. Reads #~"$HOME" from the
environment -- #~"%USERPROFILE%" on Windows, where #~"$HOME" is
not the system's notion -- and errors with #~"IoError.other" when
unset. Does not fall back to the passwd database -- set the
variable explicitly for portable behaviour across stripped
environments.


#--: hostname


Result of #~"gethostname(2)", truncated at #~"HOST_NAME_MAX"; on
Windows, the DNS host name (#~"GetComputerNameExW").
Result-wrapped because the syscall can fail in rare cases.


#--: build-commit


The build stamp this binary carries, or the empty string when its
build supplied none — so a program can report which source it was
built from. A build sets it either way:

#ul:
  [#*: define #~"ZC_BUILD_COMMIT" for the C compiler, which
    #~"zc build --cflags" passes through.]
  [#*: link a strong #~"z_build_commit" symbol, which overrides the
    weak default the native carries.]

The second route exists because the native is compiled into the
program's single translation unit: changing a define recompiles all
of it, while overriding the symbol only relinks. #~"zc" itself is
stamped that way.

#code: """
  zc build myprog --cflags '-DZC_BUILD_COMMIT="a1b2c3d4"'
  """


#--: build-date


Companion to #~"buildCommit": the date its build stamped in, or the
empty string. Set by #~"ZC_BUILD_DATE" or a strong #~"z_build_date".


#--: build-compiler


Name and version of the C compiler that built this binary, such as
#~"gcc 15.2.0". Unlike the other two it asks nothing of the build —
it reads that compiler's own predefined macros — so it is always
present.


#--: exit


Terminate the process immediately with #~"code" as the status.
Does not return; buffered output is flushed, but zerolang-level
destructors for in-scope values #b:"do not" run.

In a program that starts threads the process ends once: the first
thread to end it, with #~"os.exit" or a panic, ends it, and a thread
that does so meanwhile waits for that end. Its status is the first
thread's.

Reserve for fatal-error exits and CLI tools that want an
explicit status. Prefer a normal return path for everyday
success / failure so RAII cleanup fires.


#--: spawn


Start #~"argv" as a child process (#~"posix_spawnp"; #~"CreateProcessW"
on Windows); #~"argv[0]" is resolved via #~"PATH". stdout is redirected
to #~"outPath" and stderr to #~"errPath" (each created or truncated --
pass #~"/dev/null", #~"NUL" on Windows, to discard a stream). An #b:"empty" #~"outPath"
or #~"errPath" inherits the parent's stdout / stderr instead; when
#b:"both" are empty the child also inherits stdin -- a live terminal,
as used by #~"zc run" -- otherwise stdin is #~"/dev/null". A
non-empty #~"cwd" is the child's working directory.
The redirection files are opened in the parent #b:"before" the
child starts, so its #~"cwd" cannot change their resolution, and
close-on-exec, so no other child inherits them. Every file and socket
the runtime opens is close-on-exec, so a child sees only its three
standard streams.

The return value is the child's exit status; #~"128 + signo" for a
child a signal ended; #~"127" if exec or any setup step failed;
#~"124" if it timed out. #~"timeoutSecs"
greater than zero kills the child (#~"SIGKILL", #~"TerminateProcess")
after that many seconds; #~"0" (the default) waits indefinitely. The
timeout touches no process-wide state -- it waits on the child's pidfd
or process handle, not on a signal -- so a spawn in one thread does not
disturb another's.

#b:"On Windows" the command line is quoted so that the child's C
runtime, and #~"CommandLineToArgvW", split it back into exactly
#~"argv". A program named without an extension is looked for with
#~".exe" and #~".com" in each #~"PATH" directory. A #~".bat" or
#~".cmd" file is refused (#~"127"; #~"spawnAsync" says why): cmd.exe
runs those, by quoting rules of its own, so spawn #~"cmd /c <file>"
explicitly. Only the three standard handles are inherited. The model
mirrors #~"subprocess.run(argv, timeout=...)" -- a single
run-to-completion call, not a separate spawn / wait split.

Captured output is read back with #~"io.readText"; the child
inherits the environment, so set variables with #~"os.setEnv"
beforehand. There is no streaming / pipe interface in v1.

#--: spawn_async


Start #~"argv" like [#> to=spawn: #~"os.spawn"] -- same
#~"PATH" resolution, redirection and #~"cwd" rules -- but return
immediately with the #~"Child": its #~"pid" and the system's handle on
that very process (a pidfd on Linux where the kernel has them, the
process handle on Windows). The error says why the start failed; a
program that is not there is #~"notfound". There is no timeout
parameter: reap with [#> to=wait-any: #~"os.waitAny"] and enforce
deadlines with [#> to=monotonic-millis: #~"os.monotonicMillis"] plus
[#> to=kill: #~"Child.kill"]. Together the calls form a worker pool's
primitive set (see #~"src/ztestrunner.z" for the canonical poll loop).
Dropping a #~"Child" closes its handle and leaves the process running.

#--: wait_any


Reap one exited child among #~"children" without blocking, and mark it
reaped. A #~"pid" of #~"0" means none of them has exited. Only the
children given are reaped -- a child another part of the program
started is never taken from it -- and one already reaped is skipped.
#~"status" decodes like #~"os.spawn": the exit code; a signalled child
reports #~"128 + signo"; one [#> to=kill: #~"Child.kill"] ended reports
#~"137" on every system.

#--: kill


#~"child.kill" ends the child now: #~"SIGKILL", through its pidfd where
there is one, so no process that came to reuse the pid can be reached;
on Windows #~"TerminateProcess" with exit code #~"137", the status a
SIGKILLed child reports. It does nothing once the child is reaped.
Reap it with [#> to=wait-any: #~"os.waitAny"] afterwards.

#--: sleep_millis


Suspend the calling thread for #~"ms" milliseconds
(#~"nanosleep", restarted after #~"EINTR" so the full duration
always elapses; #~"Sleep" on Windows). Non-positive #~"ms" returns immediately.

#--: monotonic_millis


Milliseconds on the monotonic clock
(#~"CLOCK_MONOTONIC"). Only differences are meaningful -- the
epoch is arbitrary (typically boot time). This is the clock behind
#~"zc --time" and the test runner's deadlines.
